  "Question": "Consider the statement: “If a whole number \\(n\\) is not prime, then the whole number \\(n - 2\\) is not prime.” Which of the following values of \\(n\\) shows this statement to be false?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(9\\)",
    "B": "\\(12\\)",
    "C": "\\(13\\)",
    "D": "\\(16\\)",
    "E": "\\(23\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "An “if...then...” statement is false when its condition holds but its conclusion fails. Here we need a number \\(n\\) that is not prime while \\(n-2\\) is prime.\n\nFor \\(n=9\\), the factorization \\(9=3\\times3\\) shows that \\(9\\) is not prime. However,\n\\[\n9-2=7,\n\\]\nand \\(7\\) is prime because its only positive factors are \\(1\\) and \\(7\\). Thus \\(n=9\\) disproves the statement, choice A.",
